---
import LanguageServerDoc from "@components/docs/LanguageServerDoc.astro";
import DocsLayout from "@layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
---

<DocsLayout title="Java" description="Java language-server support in Axon">
  <LanguageServerDoc
    title="Java"
    description="Axon uses the Eclipse JDT Language Server for Java projects. It provides project-aware completions, diagnostics, imports, hover, definitions, references, rename, formatting, and symbols."
    server="Eclipse JDT Language Server"
    delivery="managed native server with local JDK requirement"
    activation="Starts for .java files and Java workspaces"
    overview={[
      "Java language intelligence depends on the project model. Maven, Gradle, source roots, generated files, classpaths, annotation processors, and the selected JDK all affect what the server can understand.",
      "Axon can bundle the Java language-server distribution, but a local JDK is still required for real Java projects. The JDK is part of how the server runs and how project analysis understands Java language levels.",
      "Opening the repository root or the folder that owns the build files gives the server the best chance of resolving imports, dependencies, generated code, and project structure.",
    ]}
    capabilities={[
      "Completions for classes, methods, fields, packages, imports, constructors, overrides, and generated symbols.",
      "Hover for types, signatures, Javadocs, package members, inherited methods, and class information.",
      "Diagnostics, go to definition, references, rename, organize imports, formatting, document symbols, and workspace symbols.",
      "Build-file-aware behavior for Maven and Gradle projects when the local JDK and project metadata are available.",
    ]}
    setup={[
      {
        title: "Install a JDK",
        body: "Bundling the server does not remove the JDK requirement. Java projects need a local JDK for project analysis, build behavior, language-level support, and runtime integration.",
      },
      {
        title: "Open the build root",
        body: "Open the folder that owns pom.xml, build.gradle, settings.gradle, or the project metadata. Opening only src/main/java can prevent the server from seeing dependencies and source sets.",
      },
      {
        title: "Allow indexing time",
        body: "Large Java projects can take time to import and index. Axon should show a running/indexing state and logs instead of making the editor feel frozen.",
      },
    ]}
    packaging={[
      "The Java server is packaged as a managed native/server bundle, but it expects a working local JDK.",
      "If release builds fail, check both Axon's packaged server path and the user's JDK availability. The error message should distinguish those two cases.",
    ]}
    troubleshooting={[
      {
        title: "Server fails with runtime errors",
        body: "Check whether a compatible JDK is installed and visible to Axon. A bundled server cannot analyze Java without the needed Java runtime setup.",
      },
      {
        title: "Imports do not resolve",
        body: "Open the Maven or Gradle root and make sure dependencies can be restored. Missing build metadata often appears as missing imports.",
      },
      {
        title: "Indexing takes a while",
        body: "That can be normal for large Java workspaces. Axon should expose progress and logs so users can tell whether the server is working or stuck.",
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocsLayout>
